s 3, so possible but not high probability. T9 and B9 are terminals, discarding them reduces terminals. T7 and B3 are simples, might be useful for sequences. T2 is simple but I only have one. B9 is terminal.  \nLooking at potential chows: I have T7, T8, T9 \u2013 that's a Chow sequence! Actually T7, T8, T9 form a Chow if I have T7, T8, T9. I have T7:1, T8:2, T9:1 \u2013 that's exactly T7, T8, T8, T9. So I have T7, T8, T9 which is a Chow of 7-8-9. I could potentially use that as a concealed Chow if I keep them together. But I have two T8s, so I have an extra T8. I could discard one T8 to keep the Chow T7-T8-T9. Also, I have B6 pair and B3, B9 \u2013 no sequence there. W3 and W7 are singles. So perhaps I should keep the T7-T8-T9 Chow and try to form other melds. My valid actions include Play T8, which would break the pair but keep the Chow (since I still have one T8). Or I could discard T9 to keep the T8 pair, but then I lose the Chow. The Chow is valuable for points like All Chows, Mixed Straight, etc. Given that I already have an exposed Chow in W suit, if I can get another Chow in T suit (T7-T8-T9) and one in B suit, I might aim for Mixed Straight (three chows in different suits of 123, 456, 789). I have W4-W5-W6 (that's 456 in W), and T7-T8-T9 (789 in T), so I would need a Chow of 123 in B suit to complete Mixed Straight. I have B3 and B6, B9 \u2013 no B1 or B2. But unseen B1 count is 1, B2 is 1, so very low chance. Alternatively, I could aim for Half Flush (only one suit and honors). I have a W meld, and some W singles (W3, W7), so if I discard all T and B tiles and try to get more W tiles, I could aim for Half Flush with honors. But I have no honors besides winds, and I have many non-W tiles. That seems difficult. Another pattern: All Types \u2013 requires all three suits and winds and dragons. I have W, T, B, and I have winds (W3, W7 are West Wind, which is wind), but no dragons. I would need a dragon tile. Unseen J1=4, J2=3, J3=0. J3 is exhausted, so only J1 or