it's prevailing and seat wind, so keeping it might allow pung for 4 points. Unseen W3=2, so possible.  \n- W7 is wind, but not prevailing/seat? West Wind is W3? Actually West Wind is F3? No, Wind tiles are F1 East, F2 South, F3 West, F4 North. So West Wind is F3. W3 is Character-3, not a wind. My mistake. W3 and W7 are suited tiles, not honors. So they are simples. So my hand actually has no honor tiles! I have only suited tiles. That's good for patterns like All Simples, No Honors.  \nSo reevaluate: All my tiles are suited tiles, no honors. So I can aim for All Simples (2 points) and possibly All Chows (2 points) if I get four chows. Currently I have one exposed chow (W4-W5-W6), and potential chow T7-T8-T9. If I can get two more chows, that's All Chows. Also, if I have no honors and no terminals, I get All Simples. But I have terminals: T9 and B9 are terminals (number 9). So to get All Simples, I need to discard T9 and B9. So discarding terminals is good for that pattern. Also, for All Chows, I need chows, so keeping sequences is good.  \nSo promising moves: discard a terminal tile (T9 or B9). Between T9 and B9, T9 is part of a potential Chow, so discarding T9 breaks that Chow. B9 is not part of any sequence, so discarding B9 is better to preserve the T7-T8-T9 Chow. Also, B9 is a terminal, so discarding it moves toward All Simples.  \nWhat about T2? T2 is simple, not terminal, might be useful for chow with T3-T4. Unseen T3 and T4 are plenty. So keep T2.  \nB3 is simple, useful for chow with B4-B5.  \nW3 and W7 are simples, but they are isolated; W3 could pair with W2-W4 but W2 unseen=0, W4 unseen=0? Unshown tiles: W2:0, W4:0, so both exhausted. So W3 cannot form a chow with adjacent tiles because W2 and W4 are gone. W7 could form chow with W5-W6 or W6-W8, but W5 unseen=3, W6 unseen=0, W8 unseen=1. W6 is exhausted, so W7-W8-W9? W9 unseen=2, so W7-W8-W9 possible if I get W8 and W9. But I have no W8 or W9 in hand. Unseen W8=1, low chance. So W7 is not very useful for chow. But it could be part of a pung if I get two more W7. Unseen W7=3, so possible. Similarly, W3 pung possible with unseen W3=2. So keeping them for pung potential is okay, but they are less flexible than sequences.  \nGiven that, discarding B9 seems best to preserve the T Chow and move toward All Simples.  \nOther option: discard T8 to keep the Chow but then lose a pair. But I have B6 pair, so I can afford to lose T8 pair. However, T8 is a simple tile and can be used in chows, but if I discard one T8, I still have T7, T8, T9 as Chow, and one extra T8 which is now a single. That might be okay. But then I have two singles (T8 and B9). Discarding B9 leaves me with T8 pair and Chow. So better to discard B9.  \nAlso consider tile safety: history shows B9 has been discarded? Looking at history: my history: no B9. Next player: no B9. Across: no B9. Previous: no B9. So B9 might be safe.  \nTherefore, I choose to discard B9.\n\nReason: Discarding B9 preserves the potential Chow T7-T8-T9, moves the hand toward All Simples by removing a terminal, and keeps flexibility with other tiles. B9 has limited use for sequences and is relatively safe based on discard history.\n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
